{
  "dom_id": "step-q1241",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "The inequality $x<-7$ can be expressed in interval notation as",
  "worked_solution": "The inequality $x<-7$ corresponds to all numbers less than $-7.$ A number line for this interval is shown below:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191214/assets/309a19968d017e575543f8165405dea2fe102a2d9a10d575ecf1cead17d36e07.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- There is no left endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with negative infinity as the left endpoint, not included.\n\n- The right endpoint $-7$ is *not* included in the interval.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $x<-7$ as\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-7).\n$$",
